1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:02,198
Nitrogen gas is almost inert —

2
00:00:02,198 --> 00:00:06,839
its two atoms are bound so tightly that under normal lab conditions,

3
00:00:06,839 --> 00:00:09,910
they simply refuse to separate and recombine.

4
00:00:09,910 --> 00:00:14,279
Chemist after chemist across Europe tried to force the reaction,

5
00:00:14,279 --> 00:00:15,279
and failed.

6
00:00:15,279 --> 00:00:16,279
In Germany,

7
00:00:16,279 --> 00:00:20,647
a professor named Fritz Haber decided to attack it head-on: take

8
00:00:20,647 --> 00:00:22,421
nitrogen and hydrogen gas,

9
00:00:22,421 --> 00:00:25,971
add heat and pressure, and force them into ammonia —

10
00:00:25,971 --> 00:00:28,564
a compound plants can actually absorb.

11
00:00:28,564 --> 00:00:31,977
In 1905, working at roughly 1,000 degrees Celsius,

12
00:00:31,977 --> 00:00:36,209
Haber coaxed out a conversion rate of only about five percent.

13
00:00:36,209 --> 00:00:37,301
It was progress.

14
00:00:37,301 --> 00:00:39,578
It was nowhere near enough to matter.

